Z||000000||Adam Draper||02-20-2001||10:49 PM||||When my car hits a bump or a uneven spots on the road,my cars goes to the right, Does this mean that I need a new shocks and struts? When I said my car goes to the right it goes to the right when it hits a bump(not a bigg bump smalls like sewer lid. help me||||reg||4
Z||000001||RaeQwan||02-20-2001||11:36 PM||||turn left <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by RaeQwan (edited 02-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||GT Bastard||02-21-2001||08:17 AM||||It's called bump-steer.  Common Fiero problem.  You get used to it.  Look under the front of you car on the steering rack.  You'll see a small shock mounted horizontally.  That's designed to absorb shock such as the kind you describe.  If it's leaking replace it.  It might be time for a new one.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||02-21-2001||12:49 PM||||Could also be worn out ball joints.||||reg||1
Z||000004||terryk||02-21-2001||01:58 PM||||Could be localized gravity surges.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>When I've had bump-steer it seems to go one way then back the other before coming back straight.<P>Sounds more like something is up in the front end if it always goes right. <P>Leave the car on the ground, grab the wheels and start moving them around and see how much play there is. Front ball-joints and control arm bushings would be my first guess. Then the rack.<p>[This message has been edited by terryk (edited 02-21-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||02-21-2001||07:30 PM||||Don't forget to check the cradle bushings on the 84-87 cars..... Those can cause problems that feels like the front but aren't.||||reg||1
Z||000006||terryk||02-21-2001||11:18 PM||||Theogre, how do you go about checking the cradle bushings? In or out of the car? Loaded or unloaded? Curious.||||reg||5
Z||000007||GarethWright||02-22-2001||02:51 AM||||When I put new upper and lower ball joints on my 87 GT I had bump steer from hell!  Not much fun when it wasn't happening before.  Try this:  Go get an alignment done at a reputable tire/alignment place, and tell em Specifically that you want the Toe In checked and Adjusted as well.  Some places do the Toe In as a standard part of the alignment, others don't.  This cleared it Right up for me, apparently my Old ball joints were so bad they couldn't do an alignment, but still no bump steer even then.<BR>Good Luck with it, and Stay away from Road Construction! (aka pot-hole/random speed-bump generation around here)||||reg||1
